## Project board (human+agent kanban)

**Org project #<BOARD_NUMBER> "{{ project_name }}"** (https://github.com/orgs/{{ github_owner }}/projects/<BOARD_NUMBER>) is the standing human-facing coordination surface. Cards carry **coordination state only** — title, Status, Responsible, assignee, and a link to the PR / notes file / HUMAN-REVIEW-QUEUE entry; knowledge stays in the md files. Card grain = attention-transfer events (human gates, PR-sized agent work) — never agent-internal micro-tasks. Repo labels: `human-review`, `agent-work`, `stakeholder` (decision belongs to a non-GitHub stakeholder; delivered by relay — the assignee shows them the packet and records the decision as comment-with-attribution + close). Rationale: `PROCESS.md`.

- **Tool-neutral procedures live in `process/`** (`next.md`, `kanban-check.md` + its implementation `kanban_check.sh` [read-only orientation report — the script is what `next` actually runs for its orientation passes; the md is its spec and by-hand fallback, carries no delegation policy of its own, and must stay self-contained: never make it cross-reference `next.md`, restate the rule instead], `board-setup.md`); per-tool entry points are thin wrappers: `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (Claude Code), `.opencode/command/<name>.md` (OpenCode), `.codex/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (Codex). Fallback for any tool without discovery: when the user says "next" (and only then), follow `process/next.md` directly.
- **Columns (Status):** Unshaped → Todo → Agent working → Awaiting code review (a PR/diff exists and awaits review) → Awaiting human action (decision, sign-off, or real-world action — no diff) → Done. Keep Status truthful as you work: Agent working when you start a card, Awaiting code review when its PR opens, Done on merge/close. A card left in Agent working by a session that stopped is invisible to every future session — `next`'s sync pass sweeps those back to Todo after 24 h. **Renaming/adding options via `updateProjectV2Field` regenerates ALL option IDs and silently clears every item's Status** — snapshot `item-list` first, re-apply after, and re-wire the board's built-in workflows in the UI.
- **Unshaped column = the fluid project plan**: everything we believe we'll do, as **draft items** (cheap one-liners: source + gate), NOT issues. **Shaping = converting draft → real issue** with a self-contained body, labels, assignee, Responsible, then Status → Todo. **Shaping is human-triggered: agents draft, humans ratify** — an agent shapes only when a human asks (or offers and gets a yes); never promote drafts to Todo unprompted. There is deliberately **no Blocked column**: blocked-on-a-human is Awaiting human action; blocked-on-other-work stays Unshaped with its gate named.
- **Card bodies are self-contained:** the human action, and the agent work it unblocks, must be executable without the originating conversation — exact ask, where to look, what proceeds when cleared. If a card needs the full prior chat to make sense, it isn't ready to be a card.
- **Lead with the ask — every time.** A card transfers one decision to a person who has not read the conversation that produced it, so order matters more than length. **The first line of a new card's body is the ask itself** — the question or action, in plain language, understandable without opening a file; context, reasoning and evidence come after. **The last comment on a card must open the same way**, including "nothing right now, this is FYI" when that is the truth; if a comment changes what is being asked, the new ask goes on top. Plain language means no repo context: "Confirm `edu.example.app` is the final bundle identifier" is an ask; "resolve the HRQ #13 remainder" is not — spell out acronyms and card numbers on first use. A thread whose current ask can only be reconstructed by reading every comment in order has already failed. (This is also what lets `kanban_check.sh` report each card's ask mechanically.)
- **Anything a human must read to clear a gate is committed to the repo** — review packets, transcripts, rendered artifacts — tracked in git and linked from the card by permalink. "Regenerate it with this script" is not delivery: it strands the reviewer on whichever machine produced the artifact and makes the approved thing unrecoverable later. Give a gitignore rule that would swallow a reviewable artifact an explicit un-ignore. Sensitive or personal data stays out of git regardless — build packets from synthetic or already-public content.
- **Consolidated gate cards:** where one human decision would clear several gates at once, they collapse to one card with checkboxes rather than one card each, and a note at the top of `HUMAN-REVIEW-QUEUE.md` names the current consolidations. A new review question on a consolidated topic is a checkbox plus a comment on that card, not a new card. **This consolidates gates, not work** — distinct build work always gets its own card, including work arising from a consolidated topic. Test: a human *decides* it → consolidate; an agent *does* it → card it.
- **Every card gets a GitHub assignee (a real account = the accountable human); assigning the wrong human beats assigning no one.** The `Responsible` single-select says who actually acts: AI agent (the coding agent, any tool) / one option per developer / per stakeholder / Copilot (AI reviewer).
- **Multi-developer rule: an agent *starts work* only on cards assigned to its own operator.** Board bookkeeping and cleared-gate processing are **global and claim-guarded** (lane-scoping those would make follow-on work wait for a specific human to open a session); claiming = a nonce-stamped claim comment on the issue, **arbitrated by comment ID** (claim → re-fetch → lowest ID wins; Projects fields have no atomic test-and-set), with Status/assignee as the visible signal (Agent working when starting; reassign after winning the claim when poaching; `Processed:` comments on gates). Details: `process/next.md`.
- **Signals vs payload:** a card move is the signal; the substance (decision text, approval wording, feedback) goes in an issue comment. A closed `human-review` issue with no contrary comment = approved. A human comment on a still-OPEN gate is payload too: explicit complete decision → process it and close with attribution; **change request → re-arm** (card to Agent working, do the work, reply what changed, back to Awaiting human action — never close for a human who isn't done deciding); question → answer; ambiguous → surface, never guess. Close is reserved for "my decision is complete."
- **Session pickup:** the `next` procedure (`process/next.md`, entry points above) — its orientation passes (steps 0–3) are produced by running `process/kanban_check.sh` directly in-session (read-only; deterministic, so it cannot misremember the board — it replaced delegating those passes to a local model, after delegates were measured inventing issue numbers and writing to the checkout); judgment and all writes stay with the session — **run it only when the human invokes it** (the command, or "what's next?"); never orient against the board unprompted. **The deliverable is the work, not the orientation report:** a turn ends on a PR, a merge, or a recorded blocker — never on a claim comment and a board move alone. Read a card's comments at pickup — guidance lands there. Don't double-card: a PR already tracked by an issue card in Awaiting code review gets no second card. Mid-session, watch a card you're blocked on with a background polling loop (Claude Code: the Monitor tool; other tools: a shell loop, or re-run next when you return). When no session is running, moves simply queue — the board is a mailbox, not a pager.
- **Plumbing** (token needs `project` scope — `gh auth refresh -s project`): create the issue, then `gh project item-add <BOARD_NUMBER> --owner {{ github_owner }} --url <issue-url>`, then set fields with `gh project item-edit --project-id <PROJECT_ID> --id <item-id> --field-id <field-id> --single-select-option-id <option>`. Status field `<STATUS_FIELD_ID>`: Unshaped `<OPT_UNSHAPED>` · Todo `<OPT_TODO>` · Agent working `<OPT_AGENT_WORKING>` · Awaiting code review `<OPT_CODE_REVIEW>` · Awaiting human action `<OPT_HUMAN_ACTION>` · Done `<OPT_DONE>`. Responsible field `<RESPONSIBLE_FIELD_ID>`: AI agent `<OPT_AI_AGENT>` · … (board-setup fills this whole block after creating the board). Item IDs come from `item-list`, and **truncation there is silent — the symptom is a card that simply appears not to exist**, so treat it as a hard failure rather than a caveat:

  - **Filter server-side, don't just raise the limit.** `--query "-status:Done"` cuts the fetch because it runs on the server and `totalCount` itself drops. A `jq` filter is no help — `--limit` truncates before anything client-side runs. Filter syntax is GitHub's Projects one (`-status:Done`, `status:Todo`, `status:"Awaiting human action"`, `assignee:…`).
  - **Guard every fetch**, because any limit gets outgrown eventually. Two forms, because the strong one is not always available:
    - `gh project item-list` returns `totalCount`, which stays truthful even when the item list is truncated, so compare the two: `… --format json | jq -e '.totalCount as $t | (.items|length) as $n | if $t == $n then . else error("TRUNCATED: \($n) of \($t)") end'`
    - **`gh issue list` / `gh pr list` have no `totalCount`** (asking for it errors: `Unknown JSON field`). Use the weaker but universal tripwire — *returned == limit is ambiguous, so treat it as truncated*: `n=$(… --limit 100 --json number --jq 'length'); [ "$n" -lt 100 ] || echo "TRUNCATED — raise the limit"`. It cannot tell "exactly 100" from "at least 100", which is the point: it fails toward suspicion.
  - **Never apply `-status:Done` when hunting cleared gates.** Closed gate cards live in Done, so that filter would hide precisely the human decisions the gate pass exists to process. Those come from the issue API instead — `gh issue list --label human-review --state closed` — which no board filter can affect. Open gates still come from the board.
  - Closing an issue does **not** remove its card from the board (the card stays, as Done), so close-with-comment costs nothing and remains the gate signal.
  - `item-add` is not immediately visible to `item-list`; re-query until the item appears rather than assuming the add failed, and never print success without verifying the field edits landed.

## PR flow

Commit as work completes on a feature branch, `gh pr create`, watch for the Copilot review of the current head (poll the reviews API from the local machine — never a billed-runner loop). Findings → fix and push; clean review of the head → merge. **Copilot-unavailable fallback: a quota-limit "unable to review" body is a non-review** — do NOT re-request (quota won't refresh in seconds); go straight to a **context-blind fresh-agent review** (any capable tool/model, prompt essentially only "Review PR #n in this repo"; do NOT feed it the authoring conversation) — then address findings and merge. Never make any single reviewer a hard dependency.

**Branch naming: `issue-<n>[-slug]`.** `next`'s stale-claim sweep asks "is there a remote branch for this card?" and answers it by matching that prefix — a branch named anything else reads as no branch at all, and a card with real work behind it gets swept back to Todo.

**After a merge, clean up locally — and do not trust `git branch --merged`.** `gh pr merge --squash --delete-branch` deletes only the **remote** branch. Delete the local branch and remove any worktree you made for the work in the same breath as merging. The usual "delete merged branches" idiom is actively misleading under squash merging: squashing rewrites the commits, so a merged branch is **never** an ancestor of `main` and `git branch --merged origin/main` lists only `main` itself — no feature branch ever appears, which reads as "all clean" while they pile up. Decide by PR state instead (`gh pr list --state all --json number,state,headRefName`) and delete with `git branch -D`. A worktree also **pins** its branch, so a branch sweep silently skips whatever a worktree holds — remove the worktree first (`git worktree remove <path> && git worktree prune`), then the branch, then `git remote prune origin`. **Review-scratch branches count** (a PR checked out locally to look at it): they sit on *intermediate* commits of a merged PR, so they are patch-contained but invisible to every ancestry check, and they are the easiest kind to forget. Before deleting, confirm nothing unique is there — no uncommitted changes, and every commit accounted for in some merged PR's commit list (`gh pr view <n> --json commits`); `git cherry` cannot answer this, because squashing changes the patch id. Never delete a worktree you cannot inspect, and never report as gone anything you did not verify.

## Human review queue

`HUMAN-REVIEW-QUEUE.md` holds the full writeups and packets for human gates (review materials, what each gates, effort). **Every open item there has a board card** (label `human-review`, Responsible = the human, Awaiting human action when materials are ready) — *except* items covered by a consolidated tracking card, which get a comment on that card instead (see "Consolidated gate cards" above; a note at the top of this file names the current consolidations, and the match is by **category**, not by a list of enumerated items). Check items off as the humans complete them. Anything a reviewer must read to decide is committed to the repo and linked by permalink, never regenerated on demand.

